// The tweenstack package (a working name; Jon names packages): animators that
// run over time and write the value of a field each tick. A program reaches
// it with 'import tweenstack'. It builds on keyframetracks (clips, easing,
// quaternion and colour mixing) and adds what that package leaves out: a
// registry of running animators over many targets, with handles, events and
// composition, and the kinds that depend on the value a field has now.
//
//   mut reg = Animators.make();
//   reg.setF32(LAMP, GLOW, 0.);                                     // the field's value now
//   imm a = Anim.transition(LAMP, GLOW, Value.ofF32(1.), 15.);      // BUILT: a value, nothing runs
//   imm h = reg.start(&a);                                          // STARTED: a handle
//   // every frame, where the host wants it relative to physics:
//   reg.tick(now);                                                  // or reg.step(now) ... reg.evaluate()
//   imm glow = reg.valueF32(LAMP, GLOW, 0.);
//   each ev in reg.takeEvents() { ... }                             // started, ended, repeated, marker
//
// The invariants:
//
// - **An animator is a value until it is started.** Anim is plain data
//   (storable, copyable, composable). 'start' copies it into the registry and
//   returns a u32 handle; 'stop', 'pause', 'resume', 'seek', 'scrub',
//   'setRate', 'setGoal', 'complete' act on a handle.
// - **An animator writes its field directly and leaves.** There are no
//   weights, layers or combine modes; when an animator ends it is removed and
//   the value stays where it was left. A field has one writer at a time:
//   starting an animator takes the fields it writes from whatever wrote them,
//   and the new one starts from the value the field has now, so replacing
//   one by another shows no jump. How several effects combine on one thing
//   is the author's own formula over separate values.
// - **Pure kinds are a function of time since the start; stepped kinds are
//   stepped.** A transition, keys, along and groups of them (and a spring
//   whose goal never moves) are a function of their start moment and
//   parameters: 'isPure' says so, and it decides what a network must send.
//   A chase, and a spring after its goal moved, are stepped by the host's
//   'setGoal'.
// - **The registry holds no references.** A field is (target id, property
//   key) as keyframetracks tracks are addressed, and the registry keeps only
//   its current value. The host reads it back after 'evaluate'.
// - **The host decides where a tick runs.** Time is a number the host gives;
//   'step' moves clocks and raises events, 'evaluate' writes the fields.
//
// - value.cone      Value: one field's value of any supported type (f32,
//                   Vec2, Vec3, Quat, Color), and how two mix
// - motion.cone     Easing as a value, a damped spring's velocity and
//                   settling time, a facing rotation
// - curve.cone      Curve3: polyline, Catmull-Rom and Bezier paths with an
//                   arc-length table, for 'along'
// - anim.cone       Anim: transition, keys, along, chase, spring and group,
//                   the timing fields (delay, repeat, direction, hold,
//                   rate), together / sequence / stagger / lagged / oneOf and
//                   the Timeline builder
// - engine.cone     one running animator: the phase of a node in time, the
//                   walk that writes its values, the walks that step its
//                   stateful parts and find markers and repeats
// - animators.cone  the registry: fields and their owners, start / stop /
//                   pause / seek / rate, setGoal, tick, events
//
// Conventions are keyframetracks' and geomath's: right-handed, f32, quaternions
// x y z w, colours linear sRGB (mixed in linear light unless an animator
// asks for OKLab), times in seconds (the registry's clock is f64), property
// keys u32 (keyframetracks' PROP_FIRST_USER and up for a program's own).

mod tweenstack;

import libc;
import collections use *;
import geomath use *;
import keyframetracks use *;
